## Proof

Recall the definition: a smooth map $f: M^n \to N^m$ between smooth manifolds is an **immersion** if at every point $p \in M$, the differential
$$df_p : T_p M \longrightarrow T_{f(p)} N$$
is injective.

Here $df_p$ is a linear map between the tangent spaces, with
$$\dim T_p M = n, \qquad \dim T_{f(p)} N = m.$$

A basic theorem of linear algebra states that an injective linear map $L: V \to W$ between finite-dimensional vector spaces requires $\dim V \leq \dim W$. (If $\dim V > \dim W$, then $\ker L$ has dimension at least $\dim V - \dim W > 0$ by the rank-nullity theorem, so $L$ cannot be injective.)

Applying this to $df_p$: injectivity of $df_p$ forces
$$n = \dim T_p M \leq \dim T_{f(p)} N = m.$$

This must hold at **every** point $p \in M$ (the definition of immersion requires injectivity of $df_p$ everywhere, not just at a single point). Hence a necessary condition for $f$ to be an immersion is
$$n \leq m.$$

Therefore, if $n > m$, no immersion $f: M^n \to N^m$ can exist. $\blacksquare$

